const toc = [
  { id: 'exoplanets', label: 'Planets around other stars' },
  { id: 'transit', label: 'How a transit works' },
  { id: 'depth', label: 'Transit depth and planet size' },
  { id: 'timing', label: 'Timing: period and duration' },
  { id: 'alignment', label: 'Why most planets never transit' },
  { id: 'noise', label: 'Finding tiny dips' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Discoveries and missions' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Section 21.4: Planets beyond the Solar System: Search and Discovery. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-4-planets-beyond-the-solar-system-search-and-discovery' },
  { text: 'NASA Exoplanet Archive (Caltech/IPAC): confirmed planets and discovery methods.', url: 'https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/' },
  { text: 'Winn, J. N. (2010). Transits and Occultations. In S. Seager (Ed.), Exoplanets (pp. 55–77). University of Arizona Press. arXiv:1001.2010.', url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2010' },
  { text: 'Borucki, W. J., et al. (2010). Kepler Planet-Detection Mission: Introduction and First Results. Science, 327(5968), 977–980.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="astronomy"
  topic="exoplanet-transits"
  description="Interactive exoplanet transit simulator: change planet size, orbital distance and inclination, watch the star's light dip, and see why only well-aligned orbits produce transits."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={transitQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Most planets around other stars have never been seen directly. Astronomers know they are there because, once every
      orbit, each one passes in front of its star and blocks a tiny fraction of its light. Build your own star and planet,
      send the planet across, and see how a dip of a fraction of a percent reveals a whole new world.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <TransitSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong> and watch the planet cross the star in the telescope view, while the light curve below it dips. Time slows down automatically during each transit.</li>
      <li>Try the <strong>example planets</strong>, then change the <strong>planet radius</strong>, its <strong>distance</strong> from the star (which sets the period) and the <strong>star</strong>. Compare the depth, duration and period readouts.</li>
      <li>Lower the <strong>inclination</strong> a little at a time. Watch the planet’s path move off the star in the telescope view and the side view, until the dip disappears.</li>
      <li>Add <strong>measurement noise</strong> and see which planets are still detectable. The graph below shows the dips repeating over three orbits.</li>
      <li>Click any view for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the starting planet.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="exoplanets">Planets around other stars</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>exoplanet</strong> is a planet that orbits a star other than the Sun. Thousands have now been confirmed,
    and most of them were found with the <strong>transit method</strong>. Exoplanets are extremely hard to see directly:
    they are tiny, faint and lost in the glare of their stars. Instead, astronomers look for their effects on the star.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transit">How a transit works</h2>
  <p>
    If a planet’s orbit is lined up so that it passes between its star and us, it blocks a small part of the star’s disc
    once every orbit. A telescope cannot see this happening, but it can measure the star’s total brightness very
    precisely. A graph of brightness against time is a <strong>light curve</strong>, and a transit appears as a small,
    repeating dip. To confirm a planet, astronomers look for at least three evenly spaced dips with the same depth and
    duration, and rule out other causes such as a pair of stars eclipsing each other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="depth">Transit depth and planet size</h2>
  <p>
    The fraction of light blocked is roughly the planet’s disc area divided by the star’s:
    <strong>depth ≈ (planet radius ÷ star radius)²</strong>. Jupiter crossing the Sun would dim it by about 1%; Earth by
    only about 0.008%, or 84 parts per million. So if the star’s size is known, the depth gives the planet’s size. The same
    planet produces a deeper dip around a smaller star, which is why red dwarfs are good places to look for Earth-sized
    planets.
  </p>

  <h2 id="timing">Timing: period and duration</h2>
  <p>
    The time between dips is the planet’s <strong>orbital period</strong>. Using Kepler’s third law and the star’s mass,
    the period gives the size of the orbit, which tells astronomers how much heat the planet receives. The
    <strong>duration</strong> of each transit depends on how fast the planet moves and how much of the star it crosses:
    close-in “hot Jupiters” transit for a few hours every few days, while an Earth twin would transit for about 13 hours
    once a year.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alignment">Why most planets never transit</h2>
  <p>
    Orbits are tilted at random relative to our line of sight. A transit only happens if the orbit is almost exactly
    <strong>edge-on</strong>, so that the planet passes within about one star radius of the star’s centre as seen from
    Earth. The chance of this is roughly the star’s radius divided by the orbit’s radius: about 10% for a hot Jupiter but
    only about 0.5% for an Earth twin and 0.1% for a Jupiter twin. For every planet we catch transiting, many more are
    there but unseen. Survey telescopes therefore watch tens or hundreds of thousands of stars at once.
  </p>

  <h2 id="noise">Finding tiny dips</h2>
  <p>
    Every measurement has some random scatter, or <strong>noise</strong>. From the ground, Earth’s shimmering atmosphere
    adds a lot of it, so small dips are hidden. Space telescopes avoid the atmosphere and can measure brightness to tens of
    parts per million. Combining many transits beats the noise down further, which is how planets as small as Earth have
    been found.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <code class="formula">depth ≈ (R_planet ÷ R_star)²</code>
  <code class="formula">P² = a³ ÷ M   (P in years, a in AU, M in solar masses)</code>
  <code class="formula">transit possible if a × cos i &lt; R_star + R_planet</code>
  <code class="formula">chance of a transit ≈ R_star ÷ a</code>

  <h2 id="applications">Discoveries and missions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HD 209458 b</strong> was the first exoplanet seen to transit, in 1999.</li>
    <li><strong>Kepler</strong> (2009–2018) stared at about 150,000 stars and found thousands of planets, showing that small planets are common.</li>
    <li><strong>TESS</strong> (launched 2018) surveys almost the whole sky, focusing on bright, nearby stars.</li>
    <li><strong>TRAPPIST-1</strong>, a small, cool star, has seven Earth-sized transiting planets.</li>
    <li><strong>Atmospheres:</strong> during a transit, starlight filters through the planet’s atmosphere; telescopes such as JWST read the gases in it from the light’s spectrum.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Astronomers see the planet as a black dot.”</strong> They measure only the star’s total light.</li>
    <li><strong>“A big dip means a heavy planet.”</strong> The depth measures size, not mass.</li>
    <li><strong>“If no transit is seen, there is no planet.”</strong> Most planets’ orbits are simply not lined up with us.</li>
    <li><strong>“The planet blocks all the light while it crosses.”</strong> Even Jupiter blocks only about 1%.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    Orbits are circular, and only one planet orbits each star. The star’s brightness profile uses a simple limb-darkening
    law with a fixed value, and the dip is calculated by sampling the planet’s disc at 400 points. The three star types
    and the two noise levels are illustrative round numbers, not specific stars or instruments. Real light curves also
    include starspots, flares, the faint light reflected or emitted by the planet, and dips from eclipsing binary stars
    that must be ruled out. Time is speeded up between transits and slowed down during them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A transit is a small, repeating dip in a star’s light as a planet passes in front of it.</li>
    <li>Depth ≈ (planet radius ÷ star radius)², so the dip reveals the planet’s size.</li>
    <li>The time between dips gives the orbital period; Kepler’s third law then gives the orbit’s size.</li>
    <li>Only orbits that are almost edge-on to us produce transits, so most planets are missed.</li>
    <li>Tiny dips need very precise, low-noise measurements, usually from space.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
